        "**Results and implications for modeling**\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Imbalanced:** about 55% of training samples have a rating of 5, and class 2 is the rarest (≈6.8%). The evaluation metric is **micro-F1**, which in single-label classification equals *accuracy*.\n",
        "* **The test set's distribution differs from training (an important finding):** in the first run, a model trained on the natural distribution (55% rating 5) predicted only ≈27% \"5\" on the test set (prediction distribution: 23%/14%/19%/17%/27% for 1 to 5), even though the class-5 recall on validation was ≈93%. If the test set also had 55% rating 5, this number should have been close to 55%. So the test set is roughly **balanced**. (We don't have the test labels; this inference comes from the distribution of predictions, not from direct observation.)\n",
        "  In this case, the validation accuracy on imbalanced data (78.4% in the first run) is optimistic; the expected accuracy on the balanced test set was roughly the **mean of the class recalls** (≈67%).\n",
        "* **Decision:** we **balance** both training and validation: we take an equal number of random samples from each class. Instead of loss weighting we use undersampling (we don't apply both at once, to avoid a double effect). Selecting the best checkpoint is also done on the balanced validation set, since it represents the test set.\n",
        "* **Dynamic undersampling (a fresh balanced sample in each epoch):** a fixed undersample never uses about 500 thousand samples of the high-count classes (ratings 4, 5, ...). For this reason, in each epoch we draw a *new* balanced sample: for the high-count classes, the rows from the previous epoch are not repeated (until the class pool is exhausted), and class 2, which has few samples, sees its entire pool in every epoch. The cost of each epoch stays constant but the model sees more variety in the data. (This method is known in the literature as *dynamic / per-epoch random undersampling* or per-epoch resampling; a close idea is *EasyEnsemble / balanced bagging*, which uses several balanced undersamples in several separate models instead of successive epochs.) At the end of training, the number of unique rows that were seen is printed.\n",
        "* **Sampling:** within each class, sampling is **random** with a fixed seed (42), and training/validation share no rows. (The training file is sorted by product ID, so we never take the rows at the start of the file.)\n",
        "* **Text length:** the minimum length is 200 characters (the data was already filtered), the median is ≈ 400 and the 95th percentile is ≈ 1,700 characters (about 400 tokens). Keeping all the text is expensive and the extra information is negligible; so we limit the text to `max_len` tokens (next section)."
      ]
    },
