        "Guiding principle: the transformer model has learned language from raw text itself; so **we only remove noise that carries no information and keep the sentiment signals.**\n",
        "\n",
        "| Decision | Choice | Reason |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| HTML tags and entities (`&amp;`, `<br />`) | Remove/convert | Noise; doesn't carry sentiment |\n",
        "| URL | Convert to `url` | Addresses are unique and meaningless |\n",
        "| Repeated letters (`soooooo`) | Reduce to 3 repeats | Reduces rare vocabulary but the *intensity of sentiment* (three o's) is preserved |\n",
        "| Extra whitespace | Normalize | Noise |\n",
        "| **Stop word removal** | **We don't do it** | Words like `not, no, never, but, too, very` change the meaning and direction of sentiment (\"not good\" ≠ \"good\"). The transformer needs these words for attention. (Numeric comparison in the table below) |\n",
        "| Stemming/Lemmatization | We don't do it | The subword tokenizer (BPE) handles morphology itself; removing suffixes kills tense/intensity information |\n",
        "| Lowercase | We don't do it | The RoBERTa model is case-sensitive and `WORST` / `AMAZING` are strong signals |\n",
        "| Punctuation (`!`, `?`) | We keep it | A sign of sentiment intensity (`!!!`) |\n",
        "| Spelling mistakes | We don't correct them | BPE splits an unknown word into subwords and usually preserves the meaning |\n",
        "| Adding `summary` | At the start of the input, as a sentence pair | The summary is usually the user's direct verdict (\"Great product\") |\n",
        "| Long text | **Head + tail** truncation (70% start + 30% end) | The conclusion usually comes at the start or in the last sentence; cutting only from the end loses the wrap-up |\n",
        "\n",
        "**Numeric comparison of the preprocessing choices:** below (the \"Numeric comparison of preprocessing\" section) a fast classical model (TF-IDF + linear SVM) compares these same choices with numbers so that our reasoning isn't just intuitive. Theoretical expectation: naive stop word removal reduces accuracy because negations are removed, and adding `summary` is better. **Read the actual result from the output of that cell, and if it differed from this expectation, correct the text.**"
